  The # interface is clumsy, I agree.  (Wait until you start #jumping as
a knight, or with jumping boots.  Then you'll have something to really
complain about.)  First, learn the command completion.  #l<RET> should
be enough to loot something.  It will become so automatic that wizmode
will come as a shock.  Wizard mode has extra extended commands that
begin with 'l'.  My finger macros don't work when looting in wizmode.

  You should be using enough <shifted-number> keystrokes that '#' just
isn't that much of a stretch.  Some examples.

  '@' toggles autopickup on/off
  '$' shows your current gold and debt/credit at shopkeepers.
  '(' shows tools you have in use.
  '[' shows worn armor
  '*' shows everything you're currently using.
  '_' is the travel command.
  '+' is the known spell listing/adjusting command.

  NetHack heavily rewards touch typists.  So did the old Super Star Trek
game.  Playing Super Star Trek initially gave me eyestrain because I had
to look down to hit the numbers on the keyboard.  I quickly learned to
touch-type numbers.

  Again, hard effort early pays off a lot more quickly, for much less
effort overall  Just slow down and force yourself to use them.  Maybe
focus on learning one new command/sequence a day.  Learn one short
command sequence and learn it well, then another.  Soon they'll be
calling you "Twinkle Fingers", just like me.

  You've no idea how lucky you are to have a month to practice before
the DevNull tournament.


